1
00:00:00,701 --> 00:00:01,405
Night.

2
00:00:02,146 --> 00:00:04,113
Three hundred thousand years ago.

3
00:00:04,528 --> 00:00:06,714
Fardin is hiding behind a rock.

4
00:00:07,454 --> 00:00:08,829
And he is not alone.

5
00:00:09,145 --> 00:00:10,970
Something is circling the camp.

6
00:00:11,711 --> 00:00:13,201
Then comes the sound.

7
00:00:15,172 --> 00:00:16,675
Spotted hyenas.

8
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:20,169
And Fardin just realized something terrible.

9
00:00:20,461 --> 00:00:22,452
The people around this fire can't see

10
00:00:22,472 --> 00:00:22,892
him.

11
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:26,322
But he has no idea if the hyenas can.

12
00:00:27,738 --> 00:00:29,641
But to understand how he got here,

13
00:00:29,761 --> 00:00:31,741
we need to go back a few hours.

14
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:33,851
This is Fardin.

15
00:00:34,592 --> 00:00:35,605
Normal guy.

16
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:37,242
Red hoodie.

17
00:00:37,981 --> 00:00:40,025
One very unusual watch.

18
00:00:40,282 --> 00:00:42,350
Turn the dial, and he falls back through

19
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:43,641
time as a ghost.

20
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:46,132
Nobody there can see him or hear him.

21
00:00:46,873 --> 00:00:48,260
He can only watch.

22
00:00:48,556 --> 00:00:50,817
Tonight, he set the dial further back

23
00:00:50,837 --> 00:00:51,580
than ever.

24
00:00:53,072 --> 00:00:54,961
Three hundred thousand years.

25
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:00,783
In the next twenty four hours,

26
00:01:00,891 --> 00:01:02,585
he'll see three things that changed

27
00:01:02,605 --> 00:01:04,404
how we think about our species.

28
00:01:04,674 --> 00:01:06,873
A hunt with stone tipped spears.

29
00:01:07,148 --> 00:01:08,845
A rock trick so clever,

30
00:01:08,978 --> 00:01:11,370
it lasted two hundred thousand years.

31
00:01:11,642 --> 00:01:14,006
And by tonight, the strangest discovery

32
00:01:14,026 --> 00:01:16,096
of all: who else was out there.

33
00:01:17,094 --> 00:01:17,837
Dawn.

34
00:01:18,565 --> 00:01:20,480
Fardin is standing on a dry,

35
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:22,849
rocky hillside, in what will one day

36
00:01:22,869 --> 00:01:23,844
be Morocco.

37
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:26,073
There are no countries yet,

38
00:01:26,155 --> 00:01:28,571
no farms, and no cities anywhere on

39
00:01:28,591 --> 00:01:29,166
Earth.

40
00:01:29,452 --> 00:01:32,179
Scientists call this place Jebel Irhoud.

41
00:01:32,445 --> 00:01:35,763
In 2017 researchers announced that human

42
00:01:35,783 --> 00:01:37,915
fossils found here are about three hundred

43
00:01:37,935 --> 00:01:40,387
thousand years old They are the oldest

44
00:01:40,407 --> 00:01:42,645
known fossils of our own species.

45
00:01:43,386 --> 00:01:44,773
Homo sapiens.

46
00:01:45,618 --> 00:01:46,786
And there they are.

47
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:50,447
A small group, waking up beside a cave.

48
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:52,136
Fardin's first thought?

49
00:01:52,877 --> 00:01:53,543
They look...

50
00:01:53,663 --> 00:01:55,102
surprisingly normal.

51
00:01:55,495 --> 00:01:57,217
And he's right, mostly.

52
00:01:57,957 --> 00:02:00,208
Their faces were a lot like ours.

53
00:02:00,947 --> 00:02:02,796
But the back of the skull was longer

54
00:02:02,816 --> 00:02:03,597
and lower.

55
00:02:03,837 --> 00:02:06,139
Their brains were about as big as ours,

56
00:02:06,182 --> 00:02:07,531
but shaped differently.

57
00:02:08,271 --> 00:02:10,197
The rounder brain shape we have today

58
00:02:10,217 --> 00:02:11,772
seems to have appeared later.

59
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:14,570
Here's a way to feel how long ago this

60
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:15,191
is.

61
00:02:15,464 --> 00:02:17,544
Squeeze the whole story of our species

62
00:02:17,564 --> 00:02:19,428
into one twenty four hour day.

63
00:02:20,169 --> 00:02:22,005
Farming doesn't show up until about

64
00:02:22,025 --> 00:02:23,089
eleven PM.

65
00:02:23,366 --> 00:02:24,379
The pyramids?

66
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:26,455
Eleven thirty eight.

67
00:02:27,195 --> 00:02:29,030
Almost everything in your history books

68
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:30,837
happens in the last half hour.

69
00:02:31,621 --> 00:02:33,498
And forget the Sahara you know.

70
00:02:34,238 --> 00:02:37,015
At times, North Africa was much greener,

71
00:02:37,035 --> 00:02:39,388
with grasslands, lakes and rivers.

72
00:02:39,683 --> 00:02:41,995
Gazelles and other grazing animals roamed

73
00:02:42,015 --> 00:02:42,565
here.

74
00:02:43,305 --> 00:02:45,476
Fardin tries waving at a man walking

75
00:02:45,496 --> 00:02:46,497
right past him.

76
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:48,686
Nothing.

77
00:02:49,414 --> 00:02:50,685
Not even a blink.

78
00:02:51,424 --> 00:02:53,107
He really is a ghost here.

79
00:02:53,383 --> 00:02:54,500
But that's fine.

80
00:02:55,241 --> 00:02:57,233
Because the hunt is about to begin.

81
00:02:57,973 --> 00:02:59,873
And it's nothing compared to what happens

82
00:02:59,893 --> 00:03:00,790
after dark.

83
00:03:01,725 --> 00:03:02,648
Mid morning.

84
00:03:03,389 --> 00:03:05,302
A few hunters grab their spears and

85
00:03:05,322 --> 00:03:07,740
head out, and Fardin tags along,

86
00:03:07,861 --> 00:03:09,286
three steps behind.

87
00:03:09,532 --> 00:03:12,141
Archaeologists dug up many animal bones

88
00:03:12,161 --> 00:03:14,332
at Jebel Irhoud, and the most common

89
00:03:14,352 --> 00:03:16,242
by far belonged to gazelles.

90
00:03:16,982 --> 00:03:19,645
Many carry cut marks from stone tools.

91
00:03:20,474 --> 00:03:21,655
Here's the problem.

92
00:03:22,396 --> 00:03:24,463
A gazelle can sprint at up to around

93
00:03:24,483 --> 00:03:26,077
eighty kilometers an hour.

94
00:03:26,816 --> 00:03:28,667
No human can catch one.

95
00:03:28,947 --> 00:03:30,837
So the hunters creep in low.

96
00:03:31,577 --> 00:03:32,346
Quiet.

97
00:03:33,072 --> 00:03:34,021
Downwind.

98
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:35,567
Closer.

99
00:03:35,853 --> 00:03:36,892
And closer.

100
00:03:38,132 --> 00:03:38,824
Now!

101
00:03:39,752 --> 00:03:42,066
The spear flies, and misses.

102
00:03:42,807 --> 00:03:44,736
The whole herd explodes away.

103
00:03:45,006 --> 00:03:47,370
Fardin is laughing so hard he forgets

104
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:48,390
he's invisible.

105
00:03:49,131 --> 00:03:51,974
Then he remembers, and laughs even louder.

106
00:03:53,615 --> 00:03:55,312
But the hunters don't give up.

107
00:03:56,052 --> 00:03:57,143
They spread out.

108
00:03:57,882 --> 00:03:58,728
Patient.

109
00:03:59,454 --> 00:04:00,790
Working as a team.

110
00:04:01,529 --> 00:04:03,287
Hunting like this takes planning,

111
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:04,218
and trust.

112
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:06,795
On the next try, one throw finds its

113
00:04:06,815 --> 00:04:07,442
mark.

114
00:04:08,063 --> 00:04:10,053
Some stone points from this site look

115
00:04:10,073 --> 00:04:11,703
like they were made to be fixed onto

116
00:04:11,723 --> 00:04:12,865
wooden shafts.

117
00:04:13,606 --> 00:04:15,648
Did these hunters throw their spears,

118
00:04:15,668 --> 00:04:17,068
or stab up close?

119
00:04:17,807 --> 00:04:20,057
Scientists are still debating that.

120
00:04:20,321 --> 00:04:21,911
And the strangest part of their day

121
00:04:21,931 --> 00:04:23,138
hasn't happened yet.

122
00:04:24,065 --> 00:04:26,315
Back at camp, it's lunchtime.

123
00:04:27,056 --> 00:04:29,306
And Fardin learns a rule fast.

124
00:04:30,045 --> 00:04:31,510
Nothing gets wasted.

125
00:04:31,783 --> 00:04:34,033
They slice the meat with stone flakes,

126
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:35,992
sharp enough to cut through hide.

127
00:04:36,816 --> 00:04:38,938
Then someone grabs a heavy rock,

128
00:04:39,033 --> 00:04:41,451
places a leg bone on a flat stone,

129
00:04:41,533 --> 00:04:42,971
and smashes it open.

130
00:04:43,229 --> 00:04:44,642
Inside is marrow.

131
00:04:45,383 --> 00:04:48,123
Soft, fatty, and full of energy.

132
00:04:48,862 --> 00:04:50,777
They scoop it out, and eat it.

133
00:04:51,517 --> 00:04:52,182
Raw.

134
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:54,968
Fardin looks like he's about to be sick.

135
00:04:56,703 --> 00:04:58,657
But to them, this is a treat.

136
00:04:59,398 --> 00:05:01,684
Humans and our ancestors had been cracking

137
00:05:01,704 --> 00:05:03,708
bones for marrow for more than a million

138
00:05:03,728 --> 00:05:04,419
years.

139
00:05:05,268 --> 00:05:06,578
Did they cook any of it?

140
00:05:07,318 --> 00:05:08,216
Possibly.

141
00:05:08,942 --> 00:05:10,907
Some flint tools here were burned in

142
00:05:10,927 --> 00:05:13,394
fire, and that's actually how scientists

143
00:05:13,414 --> 00:05:14,453
dated the site.

144
00:05:14,796 --> 00:05:16,825
But how often people cooked their food

145
00:05:16,845 --> 00:05:19,057
this long ago is still uncertain.

146
00:05:19,331 --> 00:05:21,672
They almost certainly ate plants too.

147
00:05:22,412 --> 00:05:24,688
Roots, seeds and fruits.

148
00:05:25,428 --> 00:05:27,743
But plants rarely survive this long,

149
00:05:27,837 --> 00:05:29,843
so most of the menu is lost.

150
00:05:30,135 --> 00:05:32,373
Fardin decides to skip lunch.

151
00:05:32,712 --> 00:05:33,571
Good call.

152
00:05:34,311 --> 00:05:35,673
Because later tonight,

153
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:37,320
he'll find out these people were not

154
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:38,675
alone on this planet.

155
00:05:39,607 --> 00:05:40,569
Afternoon.

156
00:05:41,296 --> 00:05:42,658
In the shade of the cliff,

157
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:45,428
an older man sits down with two stones.

158
00:05:46,167 --> 00:05:48,134
Fardin sits right next to him.

159
00:05:48,417 --> 00:05:50,049
This, it turns out,

160
00:05:50,157 --> 00:05:51,634
is high technology.

161
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:53,879
He doesn't just smash the rock.

162
00:05:54,619 --> 00:05:56,625
He shapes it, chip by chip,

163
00:05:56,707 --> 00:05:58,932
as if he's planning several moves ahead.

164
00:05:59,671 --> 00:06:01,571
Every strike has to land at just the

165
00:06:01,591 --> 00:06:02,501
right angle.

166
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:05,284
Then, one precise strike...

167
00:06:06,313 --> 00:06:08,358
and a ready made blade pops off,

168
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:10,020
sharp along its edges.

169
00:06:10,298 --> 00:06:12,482
Archaeologists call this the Levallois

170
00:06:12,502 --> 00:06:13,322
technique.

171
00:06:14,063 --> 00:06:15,963
The toolmaker had to picture the final

172
00:06:15,983 --> 00:06:17,486
tool inside the stone,

173
00:06:17,632 --> 00:06:18,865
before it existed.

174
00:06:19,135 --> 00:06:21,460
Methods like this were used across Africa

175
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:23,123
and beyond for more than two hundred

176
00:06:23,143 --> 00:06:24,246
thousand years.

177
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:27,206
Fire is part of life here too.

178
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,015
Burnt flint and bone tell us these people

179
00:06:30,035 --> 00:06:30,687
used it.

180
00:06:31,426 --> 00:06:33,303
Fire meant warmth, light,

181
00:06:33,347 --> 00:06:35,623
protection, and maybe cooked food.

182
00:06:35,886 --> 00:06:37,466
And far away in Kenya,

183
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:39,361
at a site called Olorgesailie,

184
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:41,510
people from around this same time carried

185
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:43,740
black obsidian rock from as far as ninety

186
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:44,966
kilometers away.

187
00:06:45,231 --> 00:06:47,981
Ninety kilometers is roughly two marathons,

188
00:06:48,001 --> 00:06:48,783
on foot.

189
00:06:49,524 --> 00:06:52,212
They also collected red and black pigments.

190
00:06:52,951 --> 00:06:54,815
Was that trade between groups?

191
00:06:55,554 --> 00:06:57,470
Some scientists think so.

192
00:06:58,222 --> 00:06:59,532
Others aren't sure.

193
00:07:00,347 --> 00:07:02,505
Either way, these were people who planned

194
00:07:02,525 --> 00:07:04,556
ahead, remembered and connected.

195
00:07:05,297 --> 00:07:07,006
Just with very different gear.

196
00:07:07,263 --> 00:07:09,178
Then the sun starts to sink.

197
00:07:09,919 --> 00:07:11,870
And Fardin is about to find out why

198
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:14,244
nobody here wanders off after dark.

199
00:07:15,176 --> 00:07:16,125
Sunset.

200
00:07:16,853 --> 00:07:18,521
The group pulls in close around the

201
00:07:18,541 --> 00:07:19,219
fire.

202
00:07:19,958 --> 00:07:22,003
Fardin finally relaxes.

203
00:07:22,290 --> 00:07:23,433
Big mistake.

204
00:07:24,817 --> 00:07:27,196
Out in the dark, shapes are moving.

205
00:07:27,456 --> 00:07:29,975
Across Africa, early humans shared the

206
00:07:29,995 --> 00:07:32,552
land with lions, leopards and spotted

207
00:07:32,572 --> 00:07:33,469
hyenas.

208
00:07:33,749 --> 00:07:34,969
And here we are.

209
00:07:35,709 --> 00:07:37,586
This is the moment from the beginning.

210
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:40,285
The hyenas circle closer.

211
00:07:41,025 --> 00:07:43,456
The adults rise, spears ready.

212
00:07:43,703 --> 00:07:45,696
Someone grabs a burning branch,

213
00:07:45,791 --> 00:07:47,436
and hurls it toward the dark.

214
00:07:47,958 --> 00:07:49,371
The eyes back away.

215
00:07:49,792 --> 00:07:51,795
Fire probably helped keep predators

216
00:07:51,815 --> 00:07:52,789
at a distance.

217
00:07:53,530 --> 00:07:55,301
It's one of the oldest safety tricks

218
00:07:55,321 --> 00:07:56,683
our species has.

219
00:07:57,493 --> 00:07:59,241
Fardin's heart is pounding.

220
00:07:59,982 --> 00:08:02,088
Then he remembers, he's the only one

221
00:08:02,108 --> 00:08:03,725
here who can literally walk through

222
00:08:03,745 --> 00:08:04,462
a wall.

223
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:06,474
He's still not going out there.

224
00:08:08,317 --> 00:08:10,580
Late at night, inside the cave,

225
00:08:10,688 --> 00:08:13,003
people lie down on beds of grass.

226
00:08:13,744 --> 00:08:15,195
Fardin tries one.

227
00:08:15,934 --> 00:08:17,489
Surprisingly comfy.

228
00:08:17,762 --> 00:08:19,355
This part isn't a guess.

229
00:08:20,096 --> 00:08:22,140
At Border Cave in South Africa,

230
00:08:22,313 --> 00:08:24,354
scientists found grass bedding about

231
00:08:24,374 --> 00:08:26,200
two hundred thousand years old.

232
00:08:26,458 --> 00:08:28,193
It was laid on top of ash,

233
00:08:28,262 --> 00:08:30,796
possibly to keep crawling insects away.

234
00:08:31,596 --> 00:08:33,947
Fardin lies awake, staring out at the

235
00:08:33,967 --> 00:08:34,775
stars.

236
00:08:35,515 --> 00:08:36,977
Then his watch buzzes,

237
00:08:36,997 --> 00:08:38,513
with one last fact.

238
00:08:39,253 --> 00:08:40,382
The biggest one.

239
00:08:40,813 --> 00:08:42,765
These people were not the only humans

240
00:08:42,785 --> 00:08:43,541
on Earth.

241
00:08:45,213 --> 00:08:47,487
Around this time, there were Neanderthal

242
00:08:47,507 --> 00:08:48,856
ancestors in Europe,

243
00:08:48,938 --> 00:08:51,124
and mysterious Denisovans in Asia,

244
00:08:51,309 --> 00:08:53,121
known mostly from their DNA.

245
00:08:53,386 --> 00:08:55,544
A small brained species called Homo

246
00:08:55,564 --> 00:08:57,544
naledi lived in South Africa.

247
00:08:58,285 --> 00:09:00,159
Another kind of archaic human lived

248
00:09:00,179 --> 00:09:01,643
in what is now Zambia.

249
00:09:02,382 --> 00:09:04,053
And on the island of Java,

250
00:09:04,148 --> 00:09:06,669
probably some of the last Homo erectus.

251
00:09:06,976 --> 00:09:08,979
At least five different kinds of humans

252
00:09:08,999 --> 00:09:10,667
may have shared the planet at the same

253
00:09:10,687 --> 00:09:11,314
time.

254
00:09:12,127 --> 00:09:14,171
Today, only one is left.

255
00:09:14,912 --> 00:09:15,616
Us.

256
00:09:16,239 --> 00:09:18,644
Scientists are still working out why.

257
00:09:19,397 --> 00:09:21,710
Much later, some of those other humans

258
00:09:21,730 --> 00:09:23,903
even had children with our ancestors.

259
00:09:24,642 --> 00:09:26,981
That's why many people alive today carry

260
00:09:27,001 --> 00:09:29,715
a little Neanderthal or Denisovan DNA.

261
00:09:30,633 --> 00:09:31,775
Dawn is coming.

262
00:09:32,516 --> 00:09:34,674
Fardin's twenty four hours are almost

263
00:09:34,694 --> 00:09:35,140
up.

264
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:37,602
His watch starts to glow.

265
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:40,104
But the dial is spinning the wrong way.

266
00:09:40,605 --> 00:09:43,564
Next time, Fardin lands in ancient Egypt,

267
00:09:43,698 --> 00:09:45,044
right in the middle of building the

268
00:09:45,064 --> 00:09:46,038
Great Pyramid.

269
00:09:46,813 --> 00:09:50,004
Subscribe, or Fardin stays lost in time.

